WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.030
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:10.530
In an earlier work, we were able to change
a photo of an already existing design according

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.900
to our taste.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.820
That was absolutely amazing.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.940
But now, hold onto your papers and have a
look at this!

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.529
Because here, we can create something out
of thin air!

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:26.900
The input in this problem formulation is an
image, and the output is an image of a different

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.060
kind.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.109
Let's call this process image translation.

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:36.839
It is translation in a sense, that for instance,
we can add an aerial view of a city as an

00:00:36.839 --> 00:00:40.420
input, and get the map of this city as an
output.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.340
Or, we can draw the silhouette of a handbag,
and have it translated to an actual, real-looking

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.519
object.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:52.719
And we can go even crazier, for instance,
day to night conversion of a photograph is

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:53.969
also possible.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.219
What an incredible idea, and look at the quality
of the execution.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:00:59.969
Ice cream for my eyes.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:05.729
And, as always, please don't think of this
algorithm as the end of the road - like all

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:10.120
papers, this is a stepping stone, and a few
more works down the line, the kinks will be

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.450
fixed, and the output quality is going to
be vastly improved.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:19.159
The technique uses a conditional adversarial
network to accomplish this.

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:24.600
This works the following way: there is a generative
neural network that creates new images all

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.530
day, and a discriminator network is also available
all day to judge whether these images look

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.280
natural or not.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.741
During this process, the generator network
learns to draw more realistic images, and

00:01:37.741 --> 00:01:42.149
the discriminator network learns to tell fake
images from real ones.

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:46.950
If they train together for long enough, they
will be able to reliably create these image

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.869
translations for a large set of different
scenarios.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:54.789
There are two key differences that make this
piece of work stand out from the classical

00:01:54.789 --> 00:02:00.210
generative adversarial networks:
One - both neural networks have the opportunity

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.229
to look at the before and after images.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:09.039
Normally we restrict the problem to only looking
at the after images, the final results.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:15.239
And two - instead of only positive, both positive
and negative examples are generated.

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:21.019
This means that the generator network is also
asked to create really bad images on purpose

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:25.980
so that the discriminator network can more
reliably learn the distinction between flippant

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.659
attempts and quality craftsmanship.

00:02:28.659 --> 00:02:32.819
Another great selling point here is that we
don't need several different algorithms for

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:38.459
each of the cases, the same generic approach
is used for all the maps and photographs,

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:41.360
the only thing that is different is the training
data.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.090
Twitter has blown up with fun experiments,
most of them include cute drawings ending

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.700
up as horrifying looking cats.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.019
As the title of the video says, the results
are always going to be stunning, but sometimes,

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:57.030
a different kind of stunning than we'd expect.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:01.040
It's so delightful to see that people are
having a great time with this technique and

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.170
it is always a great choice to put out such
a work for a wide audience to play with.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.030
And if you got excited for this project, there
are tons, and I mean tons of links in the

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:15.549
video description, including one to the source
code of the project, so make sure to have

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:20.110
a look and read up some more on the topic,
there's going to be lots of fun to be had!

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.439
You can also try it for yourself, there is
a link to an online demo in the description

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:29.480
and if you post your results in the comments
section, I guarantee there will be some amusing

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.480
discussions.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.829
I feel that soon, a new era of video games
and movies will dawn where most of the digital

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:38.270
models are drawn by computers.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:43.290
As automation and mass-producing is a standard
in many industries nowadays, we'll surely

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.819
be hearing people going: "Do you remember
the good old times when video games were handcrafted?

00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:51.140
Man, those were the days!".

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.700
If you enjoyed this episode, make sure to
subscribe to the series, we try our best to

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.000
put out two of these videos per week.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.689
We would be happy to have have join our growing
club of Fellow Scholars and be a part of our

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:06.900
journey to the world of incredible research
works such as this one.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

